blocks.code:
You directly manipulate the DOM.
const button = document.querySelector("button");
button.addEventListener("click", () => {
document.querySelector("h1").textContent = "Clicked!";
});
You're telling the browser how to change the UI.

You describe what the UI should look like based on data.
function App() {
const [clicked, setClicked] = useState(false);
return (
<>
<h1>{clicked ? "Clicked!" : "Not clicked"}</h1>
<button onClick={() => setClicked(true)}>
Click
</button>
</>
);
}
You don't manually write:
document.querySelector(...)
React handles the DOM updates.

